---

<BaseLayout
  title="Test your agent"
  description="Snapshot your agent's tool calls and fail CI the instant a prompt or model change makes it call the wrong tool, with the wrong arguments, in the wrong order. Record, approve, replay — and catch failed calls, leaked credentials and forbidden calls. Run agents against MCP servers, compare the runs, and re-score them later."
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="For agent builders"
    title="Test your <span class='brand-text'>agent</span>"
    sub="Snapshot your agent's tool calls and fail CI the instant a prompt or model change makes it call the wrong tool, with the wrong arguments, in the wrong order. No hand-written expected-call files, no YAML."
  >
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Button href={url('quickstart')} variant="primary">Quickstart</Button>
      <Button href={site.docs} variant="secondary" external>API docs →</Button>
    </div>
  </PageHeader>

  <!-- The 60-second loop -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1.05fr_1fr]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Snapshot testing</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Record → approve → replay</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          Stop hand-writing expected tool calls. Record your agent's behavior once, approve it, and
          replay it forever — Jest snapshots, for agents. Your agent's own behavior becomes the
          regression test.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          <code class="rounded bg-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-brand">toolscore init</code>
          detects your framework and scaffolds a passing pytest suite plus a CI workflow. The
          <code class="rounded bg-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-brand">toolscore_snapshot</code>
          fixture ships with the package — no plugin registration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="the whole loop" lang="bash" code={initSnippet} />
    </div>

    <div class="wrap mt-12 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
      {
        snapshotSteps.map((s) => (
          <div class="card flex flex-col p-6">
            <span class="inline-flex h-9 w-9 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-brand/30 bg-brand/10 font-mono font-bold text-brand">
              {s.n}
            </span>
            <h3 class="mt-4 text-lg font-semibold text-ink">{s.title}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 flex-1 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{s.body}</p>
            <div class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-line/80 bg-bg/50 px-3 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] text-brand">
              {s.code}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <div class="wrap mt-6">
      <CodeBlock title="test_agent.py" lang="python" code={fixtureSnippet} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- eval -> grade -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Or evaluate a trace</p>
        <h2 class="h2">A–F grade + the same "Top issues to fix"</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          Already have a captured trace and a gold standard? <code
            class="rounded bg-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore eval</code
          > scores it deterministically and gives you the same graded verdict — selection accuracy,
          argument F1, sequence accuracy, and redundancy, blended into one score.
        </p>
        <InstallCommand class="mt-6" cmd="toolscore eval gold.json trace.json" />
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="failure output — lands in your pytest log" lang="text" code={failureSnippet} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Behavior and safety -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">What the score hides</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Catch agents that give up after an error</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          A real run: a small agent asked to read a config file through the official filesystem MCP
          server tried a path outside its sandbox, got <em>Access denied</em>, and gave up without
          calling <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">list_allowed_directories</code>. Its one
          call used the right tool, so the score alone reads 90%. Toolscore shows what actually
          happened: neither required call succeeded.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          That output is from Toolscore 1.10. Since 1.11 the honesty checks also flag that the run
          ended right after the failed call (<code class="font-mono text-brand">done-after-error</code>),
          and note that the session holds no final answer, so disclosure cannot be checked.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          Add <code class="font-mono text-brand">--weight required_call_recall=0.3</code> and this run
          grades D. Sessions like this one come from
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">toolscore mcp record</code>; OpenTelemetry exports work
          the same way.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="real output — recorded MCP session" lang="text" code={gaveUpSnippet} />
    </div>

    <div class="wrap mt-12 grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Guardrails in CI</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Block destructive commands and leaked keys</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          Every evaluation reports failed calls, retries of a failed call with the same arguments,
          and credentials passed into tool arguments (shown redacted). Add rules for calls your agent
          must never make — by tool, and by argument with
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">$regex</code>,
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">$contains</code> or
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">$one_of</code> — and fail the build when one
          happens.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          The same rule in a test:
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">expect(agent).on(prompt).does_not_call("run_shell", command=Contains("rm -rf"))</code>
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="examples/guardrails" lang="text" code={guardrailsSnippet} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Honesty -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Did the agent tell the truth?</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Honesty checks on every trace</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          Correct tool calls are not enough. Toolscore reads the agent's final answer from the trace
          (OpenAI, Anthropic, Responses, LangGraph, Claude Agent SDK, OpenTelemetry) and compares it
          with what the tools returned: success claimed after a failure, a run that ends right after
          an error, a warning the answer leaves out, a code-execution tool used to get around a
          refusal, and faults injected by <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">mcp proxy --inject</code>
          that the answer hides.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          Each run gets a <code class="font-mono text-brand">false_success</code> summary. Without a
          final answer the checks report <em>unknown</em>, never pass.
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">--fail-on-honesty</code> turns error findings into a CI gate.
          Deterministic patterns, not a model: the guide lists what they can and cannot see.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm">
          <a class="text-brand hover:underline" href={`${site.docs}honesty_checks.html`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Honesty checks guide →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="examples/honesty — a hand-written trace in the shape of browser-use #6068" lang="text" code={honestySnippet} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Contracts v2 + tri-state evidence -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">More than one right answer</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Contracts: required, optional, forbidden</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          A gold file lists the calls an agent must make. A contract (version 2) also says which calls
          are <em>optional</em>, which are <em>forbidden</em>, and where more than one path is fine:
          a step with <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">alternatives</code> passes on any of them. The highest-scoring
          alternative wins; ties go to the one declared first.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          Here the agent reserved a seat instead of booking: the contract accepts that path, keeps the
          optional weather check out of the score, and still reports the extra
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">seat</code> argument. Against the old gold file the same trace grades F.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          <span class="text-ink">Tri-state evidence.</span> Every check is
          <em>pass</em>, <em>fail</em> or <em>unknown</em>, and unknown is never counted as a pass. The
          console prints the tally on every run.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <CodeBlock title="examples/contracts/contract.json" lang="json" code={contractJson} />
        <CodeBlock title="real output, narrowed" lang="text" code={contractEval} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Receipts and regrade -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Record once, re-score forever</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Receipts and <span class="font-mono">regrade</span></h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">--receipt</code> saves what was evaluated: SHA-256 hashes of the trace and
          the gold file, the settings and weights, and the result. <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore regrade</code> checks the trace is unchanged and
          scores it again with a new contract or weights. No agent call, no cost.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          The same works for harness runs:
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">toolscore regrade runs.jsonl --spec offline.yaml</code>. Inspired by
          <a class="text-brand hover:underline" href={site.aef} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Eval Flow</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <CodeBlock title="examples/contracts" lang="bash" code={receiptSnippet} />
        <CodeBlock title="real output — 38.3% F to 95.7% A, nothing re-run" lang="text" code={regradeOutput} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Fluent expect() API -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Prefer explicit expectations?</p>
        <h2 class="h2">The fluent <span class="font-mono">expect()</span> API</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          It reads like the sentence you'd say out loud. Chain
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">.calls()</code>,
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">.then_calls()</code>, and
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">.does_not_call()</code> — and loosen exactly
          the arguments you choose with matchers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="fluent assertions" lang="python" code={expectSnippet} />
    </div>

    <!-- Matchers table -->
    <div class="wrap mt-12">
      <h3 class="mb-4 text-center text-lg font-semibold text-ink">Matchers — flexible where it matters</h3>
      <div class="card scrollx overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-muted">
              <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-medium">Matcher</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-medium">Matches</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-medium">Example</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              matchers.map((m) => (
                <tr class="border-b border-line/60 last:border-0">
                  <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-brand">{m.name}</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-3 text-muted">{m.matches}</td>
                  <td class="px-5 py-3 font-mono text-[12px] text-ink">{m.example}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Framework auto-detect -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Native everywhere — zero glue</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Pass raw responses straight in</h2>
        <p class="lead mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl">
          Toolscore auto-detects the format — no manual extraction. The same is true in
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">evaluate()</code>,
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">expect()</code>, and the snapshot fixture.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-10 grid max-w-5xl grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-5">
        {
          frameworks.map((f) => (
            <div class="card flex flex-col items-center justify-center px-3 py-5 text-center">
              <span class="font-semibold text-ink">{f.name}</span>
              <span class="mt-1 font-mono text-[10px] text-faint">{f.detail}</span>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 text-center text-sm text-muted">
        Plus pytest-native fixtures, <code class="font-mono text-brand">@toolscore.cases()</code>
        data-driven tests, async support, and a CI gate — all in one package.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Agent harness -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)]">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Agent harness</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Run your agent against a real MCP server</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore agent run spec.yaml</code> runs every task x arm x repetition: an
          LLM agent (OpenAI Responses API, Anthropic Messages, or a scripted provider for offline
          runs) against any MCP server, over stdio or HTTP. Each run is scored against the task's expected
          calls and the honesty checks.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
          <li><span class="text-ink">Guard.</span> Each tool call is allowed or denied; a denied call goes back to the agent as a tool error and is logged.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Hard budget.</span> Checked before every model call. An unknown price is refused unless you allow it, and its cost then shows as unknown, never $0.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Limits and order.</span> Max turns and calls per run; cells run in a shuffled order fixed by the seed.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Resumable.</span> One JSONL row per run; run it again and completed cells are skipped.</li>
          <li><span class="text-ink">Build check.</span> The server's <code class="font-mono text-brand">serverInfo</code> name and version must match the spec.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          In this offline spec the guard denies every delete. <code class="font-mono text-brand">careful</code> says so and
          passes; <code class="font-mono text-brand">sloppy</code> answers "Done: deleted 'old' successfully." and fails the
          honesty check.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock title="examples/agent_harness/offline.yaml (narrowed)" lang="yaml" code={harnessSpec} />
    </div>
    <div class="wrap mt-6">
      <CodeBlock title="real output — scripted provider, $0" lang="text" code={harnessOutput} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Comparing runs -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Comparing runs</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Is the difference real?</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore compare --runs runs.jsonl</code> reports N per variant with
          pass, fail, error and unknown counts. Errored runs stay in the denominator. Pass rates get
          Wilson 95% intervals, differences get Fisher's exact test, and
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">pass^k</code> shows how often a task passes k times in a row. When the
          intervals overlap or N is too small, the verdict is <em>not distinguishable</em>.
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">--protocol</code> applies decision rules you declared before the runs and
          prints the file's hash.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
          <code class="font-mono text-brand">toolscore select</code> picks a variant by lexicographic, weighted or Pareto
          objectives and prints the Pareto front (selection policies inspired by Agent Eval Flow).
          These are light statistics for quick checks. For a full study (design, pre-registration,
          grading, reports) use
          <a class="text-brand hover:underline" href={site.aef} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Eval Flow</a>:
          Toolscore gives the tool-level truth, Agent Eval Flow the study-level truth.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        <CodeBlock title="real output — 6 runs per arm" lang="text" code={compareOutput} />
        <CodeBlock title="real output" lang="text" code={selectOutput} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Doctor + export -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Before you pay</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Preflight with <span class="font-mono">doctor</span></h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore doctor --spec spec.yaml</code> checks everything before the first
          paid call: packages, API keys (by name only, never the value), prices, the worst-case cost
          against the budget, and that the server starts with the expected build. It exits 1 if
          anything fails. Inspired by Agent Eval Flow's preflight.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Hand the runs on</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Neutral run records</h2>
        <p class="lead mt-4">
          <code class="font-mono text-sm text-brand">toolscore export --format aef</code> writes versioned run records: requested
          and completed calls paired by call id (a denied call says so), explicit unknown coverage,
          and SHA-256 hashes, plus a manifest. Ready for Agent Eval Flow or any other analysis.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock class="mt-6" title="real output" lang="text" code={exportOutput} />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap mt-8">
      <CodeBlock title="real output — a paid spec, stopped before any model call" lang="text" code={doctorOutput} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    heading="Make your agent's behavior a regression test"
    sub="pip install tool-scorer, then toolscore init. Record once, approve, and replay forever."
  />
</BaseLayout>
